-- Add no-XP note comprehension checks to the existing question bank.
-- Questions are scoped by curriculum level/group/node and inserted idempotently by text.
-- They are explicitly excluded from formal quizzes, reviews, challenges and XP flows.
begin;
with seed(level_name,group_name,unit_name,question_text,option_a,option_b,option_c,option_d,correct_option,explanation,difficulty,concept_name,subtopic,learning_objective) as (
values
('Ordinary Biology','Form 1','Cell membrane','What is the typical arrangement of phospholipids in a cell membrane?','A bilayer with hydrophilic heads facing watery environments and hydrophobic tails inward','A single layer with tails facing the cytoplasm','A rigid sheet of cellulose','A layer made only of proteins','A','The bilayer places hydrophilic heads next to water and hydrophobic tails away from it.','easy','Cell membrane','Membrane structure','Describe the phospholipid bilayer'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 1','Cell membrane','Facilitated diffusion differs from active transport because facilitated diffusion:','Always moves substances against a gradient','Uses ATP directly in every case','Uses membrane proteins to move substances down a gradient without direct ATP use','Occurs only in the nucleus','C','Facilitated diffusion uses channels or carriers but still moves down the concentration or electrochemical gradient.','easy','Cell membrane','Membrane transport','Compare facilitated diffusion and active transport'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 1','Cell membrane','Osmosis is best described as net movement of:','Solute from low to high concentration through any membrane','Water across a selectively permeable membrane from higher to lower water potential','Proteins through the cell wall using ATP','Oxygen into mitochondria only','B','Osmosis is net water movement down a water-potential gradient across a selectively permeable membrane.','medium','Cell membrane','Osmosis','Define osmosis'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 1','The Five Kingdoms','In the traditional five-kingdom school system, which group consists of prokaryotes?','Plantae','Animalia','Fungi','Monera','D','Monera traditionally groups bacteria and other prokaryotes, whose cells lack a membrane-bound nucleus.','easy','Five Kingdoms','Kingdom characteristics','Distinguish prokaryotes and eukaryotes'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 1','The Five Kingdoms','How do fungi commonly obtain nutrients?','By ingesting food into a digestive cavity','By absorbing dissolved nutrients from organic material','Only by photosynthesis','By fixing nitrogen in chloroplasts','B','Fungi secrete digestive enzymes and absorb soluble products from their surroundings.','easy','Five Kingdoms','Fungi','Describe fungal nutrition'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 1','The Five Kingdoms','Why can modern classification differ from the traditional five-kingdom system?','Molecular evidence has refined hypotheses about evolutionary relationships','All organisms have become multicellular','Cell structure is no longer studied','Scientific names are no longer used','A','DNA and other molecular evidence has helped refine the classification of major lineages, including prokaryotes.','medium','Five Kingdoms','Modern classification','Explain how new evidence changes classification'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 2','Factors Affecting Photosynthesis','A rise in light intensity no longer increases photosynthetic rate. What is the most likely explanation?','The plant no longer needs water','Light is always toxic at any intensity','Another factor, such as carbon dioxide or temperature, is now limiting','Photosynthesis has changed into respiration','C','Once light is no longer limiting, another required factor can determine the rate.','easy','Photosynthesis','Limiting factors','Interpret a plateau in a photosynthesis graph'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 2','Factors Affecting Photosynthesis','The oxygen released during photosynthesis originates directly from:','Water split in the light-dependent reactions','Carbon dioxide released by respiration','Glucose broken down in the Calvin cycle','Chlorophyll molecules','A','Water splitting supplies electrons and releases oxygen during the light-dependent reactions.','medium','Photosynthesis','Light-dependent reactions','Identify the source of photosynthetic oxygen'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 2','Factors Affecting Photosynthesis','When testing light intensity with pondweed, which approach best improves fairness?','Change light, temperature and carbon dioxide together','Use a different plant for every measurement','Measure one bubble once and stop','Keep temperature and carbon dioxide conditions controlled while changing light intensity','D','Changing one independent variable and controlling other factors makes the effect easier to interpret.','medium','Photosynthesis','Practical investigation','Plan a fair test of photosynthesis'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 2','Digestive Enzymes','Which enzyme begins the digestion of starch in the human digestive system?','Lipase','Amylase','Pepsin','Maltase only in the stomach','B','Amylase hydrolyses starch into smaller carbohydrates, including maltose and short chains.','easy','Digestion','Carbohydrate digestion','Match amylase to its substrate'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 2','Digestive Enzymes','What is the main role of bile in fat digestion?','It hydrolyses peptide bonds','It converts glucose into glycogen','It emulsifies fat droplets, increasing the surface area for lipase','It is the enzyme that digests starch','C','Bile is not an enzyme; it emulsifies fat into smaller droplets for lipase to act on.','easy','Digestion','Fat digestion','Explain emulsification'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 2','Digestive Enzymes','Proteases ultimately help break dietary proteins down into:','Amino acids','Fatty acids only','Starch molecules','Glycerol only','A','Proteases hydrolyse peptide bonds; further digestion produces amino acids for absorption.','easy','Digestion','Protein digestion','Identify the products of protein digestion'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 3','Reflex Actions','Which sequence best represents a typical spinal reflex arc?','Motor neurone → receptor → sensory neurone → effector','Effector → brain → receptor → sensory neurone','Receptor → sensory neurone → central relay → motor neurone → effector','Receptor → motor neurone → sensory neurone → effector','C','The receptor detects the stimulus; sensory input reaches the CNS, then motor output activates an effector.','medium','Reflex Actions','Reflex arc','Describe the pathway of a reflex'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 3','Reflex Actions','The main role of a sensory neurone in a reflex arc is to:','Carry impulses from the CNS to a muscle','Carry impulses from a receptor towards the central nervous system','Secrete digestive enzymes','Contract as the effector','B','Sensory neurones carry information from receptors towards the CNS.','easy','Reflex Actions','Neurones','Identify sensory neurone function'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 3','Reflex Actions','Why can a withdrawal reflex begin before a person has consciously decided to move?','It does not involve nerve cells','It is controlled only by hormones','Muscles respond without any signal','A rapid neural circuit can activate motor output before full conscious processing','D','A protective reflex circuit can initiate movement quickly; the brain can process the sensation in parallel.','medium','Reflex Actions','Protective responses','Explain the speed of reflex actions'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 4','Ecosystems','In a food-chain diagram, an arrow normally points from:','The organism being eaten to the organism obtaining the energy','The top predator to the sun','A consumer to the mineral soil only','The decomposer to every producer','A','Arrows indicate the direction of energy transfer from food to the organism consuming it.','easy','Ecosystems','Food chains','Interpret food-chain arrows'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 4','Ecosystems','Why is less energy usually available at higher trophic levels?','Energy is created by each predator','All energy is recycled as matter','Organisms use energy in respiration and lose energy as heat, waste and uneaten material','Producers contain no chemical energy','C','Energy transfer is inefficient because energy is used and dissipated at each trophic level.','medium','Ecosystems','Energy flow','Explain energy transfer between trophic levels'),
('Ordinary Biology','Form 4','Ecosystems','What is an important role of decomposers in an ecosystem?','They stop all energy flow','They break down dead material and return mineral nutrients to the environment','They make sunlight from minerals','They prevent nutrient cycling','B','Bacteria and fungi decompose organic matter, allowing nutrients to be reused by producers.','easy','Ecosystems','Decomposition','Explain the role of decomposers'),
('Advanced Biology','Form 5','Membrane Transport','Which statement best describes active transport?','It always moves water only','It requires no energy under any conditions','It occurs only through the phospholipid bilayer without proteins','It uses an energy source to move a substance against its gradient','D','Active transport can move substances against a gradient by coupling movement to an energy source.','easy','Membrane Transport','Active transport','Explain active transport'),
('Advanced Biology','Form 5','Membrane Transport','Why can facilitated diffusion reach a maximum rate at high solute concentration?','All molecules stop moving','The membrane becomes completely impermeable','The available channel or carrier proteins can become occupied','ATP is always depleted','C','Transport can saturate when a limited number of transport proteins are occupied.','medium','Membrane Transport','Carrier saturation','Explain saturation in facilitated diffusion'),
('Advanced Biology','Form 5','Membrane Transport','In an osmosis experiment, percentage mass change is calculated as:','(Final mass − initial mass) ÷ initial mass × 100','Initial mass ÷ final mass × 100','Final mass − initial mass without dividing','Initial mass + final mass × 100','A','Percentage change compares the difference with the initial value and multiplies by 100.','medium','Membrane Transport','Osmosis practical','Calculate percentage mass change'),
('Advanced Biology','Form 6','Gene Expression','Transcription is the process in which:','A ribosome assembles amino acids directly from DNA','RNA is synthesised using a DNA template','A protein is copied into DNA in all cells','Lipids are converted into RNA','B','RNA polymerase synthesises RNA using a DNA strand as the template.','easy','Gene Expression','Transcription','Define transcription'),
('Advanced Biology','Form 6','Gene Expression','During translation, which molecule carries an amino acid and pairs its anticodon with an mRNA codon?','DNA polymerase','RNA polymerase','Transfer RNA (tRNA)','A phospholipid','C','Transfer RNA carries an amino acid and uses its anticodon to pair with a complementary mRNA codon.','easy','Gene Expression','Translation','Describe the role of tRNA'),
('Advanced Biology','Form 6','Gene Expression','A deletion of one nucleotide within a coding sequence is likely to cause a frameshift because:','Every deletion automatically stops transcription','The ribosome reads only pairs of bases','The deleted nucleotide is always replaced by RNA processing','Codons are read in triplets, so the reading frame downstream can change','D','An insertion or deletion not divisible by three can alter downstream codon grouping.','medium','Gene Expression','Mutations','Explain a frameshift mutation'),
('Pharmacy','Certificate','Pharmacokinetics','Bioavailability is the fraction of an administered dose that:','Reaches systemic circulation unchanged','Binds permanently to plasma proteins','Is excreted unchanged before absorption','Is converted to a metabolite in the stomach only','A','Bioavailability describes the fraction reaching systemic circulation unchanged; intravenous administration is 100% by definition.','easy','Pharmacokinetics','Bioavailability','Define bioavailability'),
('Pharmacy','Certificate','Pharmacokinetics','Drug clearance is best described as:','The total dose taken by a patient','The time needed for absorption only','The volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time','The percentage of a dose that binds a receptor','C','Clearance relates the rate of drug elimination to the plasma concentration.','medium','Pharmacokinetics','Clearance','Define drug clearance'),
('Pharmacy','Certificate','Pharmacokinetics','Why can oral bioavailability be lower than intravenous bioavailability?','Oral medicines never dissolve','Some dose may be incompletely absorbed or metabolised before reaching systemic circulation','Intravenous medicines undergo intestinal absorption','All oral medicines are destroyed by the lungs','B','Incomplete absorption and first-pass metabolism can reduce the fraction reaching systemic circulation unchanged.','medium','Pharmacokinetics','First-pass metabolism','Explain factors affecting oral bioavailability'),
('Pharmacy','Certificate','Drug Interactions','Inhibition of an enzyme that normally metabolises a medicine may:','Always eliminate every adverse effect','Always lower drug concentration','Have no possible effect on exposure','Increase exposure to the affected medicine, depending on the pathway','D','Enzyme inhibition can reduce metabolism and raise exposure for susceptible substrate medicines.','medium','Drug Interactions','Pharmacokinetic interactions','Explain enzyme inhibition interactions'),
('Pharmacy','Certificate','Drug Interactions','Two medicines both causing sedation are an example of a possible:','Pharmacodynamic interaction with additive effects','Change in DNA replication only','Reduction in tablet size','Guaranteed absence of interaction','A','Their effects on the central nervous system may add together even if drug concentrations do not change.','easy','Drug Interactions','Pharmacodynamic interactions','Recognise additive pharmacodynamic effects'),
('Pharmacy','Certificate','Drug Interactions','A complete medication reconciliation should include:','Prescription medicines only','Only medicines taken yesterday','Prescription, over-the-counter, herbal and supplement products','Only medicines supplied by one pharmacy','C','A complete list helps identify interactions, duplication and other medicine-related risks.','easy','Drug Interactions','Medication reconciliation','Describe a complete medication history'),
('Pharmacy','Diploma','Concentration and Dilution','A solution contains 500 mg in a final volume of 10 mL. What is its concentration?','5 mg/mL','50 mg/mL','100 mg/mL','500 mg/mL','B','Concentration = mass ÷ final volume = 500 mg ÷ 10 mL = 50 mg/mL.','easy','Concentration and Dilution','Concentration','Calculate mass-per-volume concentration'),
('Pharmacy','Diploma','Concentration and Dilution','For a simple dilution where the amount of solute is conserved, which equation is used?','C₁ + V₁ = C₂ + V₂','C₁ ÷ V₁ = C₂ ÷ V₂ in every case','C₁V₁ = C₂V₂','C₁C₂ = V₁V₂','C','The dilution equation equates the amount of solute before and after dilution when no solute is lost.','medium','Concentration and Dilution','Dilution equation','Apply the dilution equation'),
('Pharmacy','Diploma','Concentration and Dilution','When using C₁V₁ = C₂V₂, V₂ represents:','The volume of stock used only','The volume of solvent added only','The empty container volume','The final total solution volume','D','V₂ is the final total solution volume, not simply the volume of diluent added.','medium','Concentration and Dilution','Final volume','Interpret variables in the dilution equation'),
('Pharmacy','Degree','Structure-Activity Relationships','A bioisostere in medicinal chemistry is a group or atom used to:','Replace another group while attempting to retain useful biological or physicochemical properties','Guarantee that a medicine has no adverse effects','Prove that two compounds have identical clinical outcomes','Make a molecule insoluble in every formulation','A','Bioisosteric replacement explores whether a structural change can preserve key interactions while altering properties.','medium','Structure-Activity Relationships','Bioisosteres','Explain bioisosteric replacement'),
('Pharmacy','Degree','Structure-Activity Relationships','Selectivity describes a medicine''s:','Maximum dose in milligrams','Preference for one biological target over other targets at relevant concentrations','Rate of tablet disintegration only','Ability to dissolve in water only','B','Selectivity concerns relative activity at desired and undesired targets and is concentration-dependent.','medium','Structure-Activity Relationships','Selectivity','Define target selectivity'),
('Pharmacy','Degree','Structure-Activity Relationships','Why is increasing lipophilicity not always beneficial during drug optimisation?','It always prevents membrane crossing','It always increases aqueous solubility','It may improve membrane passage but reduce aqueous solubility or increase non-specific binding','It guarantees increased efficacy','C','Medicinal chemistry optimisation balances permeability, solubility, binding and other properties.','medium','Structure-Activity Relationships','Physicochemical properties','Explain trade-offs in lipophilicity')
)
insert into public.quiz_questions
(question_text,option_a,option_b,option_c,option_d,correct_option,explanation,difficulty,is_active,unit_id,concept_name,subtopic,learning_objective,status,version,is_note_practice)
select s.question_text,s.option_a,s.option_b,s.option_c,s.option_d,s.correct_option,s.explanation,s.difficulty,true,u.id,s.concept_name,s.subtopic,s.learning_objective,'published',1,true
from seed s
join public.curriculum_levels l on l.display_name=s.level_name
join public.curriculum_groups g on g.level_id=l.id and g.name=s.group_name
join public.curriculum_units u on u.group_id=g.id and u.name=s.unit_name
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.quiz_questions existing
  where existing.unit_id=u.id
    and lower(trim(existing.question_text))=lower(trim(s.question_text))
);
commit;